WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.785
A class file is a byte array with a fixed layout.

2
00:00:05.785 --> 00:00:08.367
The first four bytes are always CAFEBABE.

3
00:00:08.567 --> 00:00:14.138
Then the minor and major version: 0x41 is 65, which means Java 21.

4
00:00:14.138 --> 00:00:17.092
Then the size of the constant pool.

5
00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:22.661
In the pool, each entry starts with a tag, and the tag decides how many bytes follow.

6
00:00:22.867 --> 00:00:26.436
So to find anything after the pool, you walk every entry.

7
00:00:26.667 --> 00:00:29.199
And every JDK adds a major version.

8
00:00:29.199 --> 00:00:33.613
Frameworks shipped their own ASM, and a release for each one.

9
00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:40.527
Since JDK 24, the JDK has its own library for it: the ClassFile API.

10
00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:46.911
Its parse method turns the bytes into a ClassModel: an immutable description of the class.

11
00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:51.705
A model is a sequence of elements: methods, fields and attributes.

12
00:00:51.933 --> 00:00:57.380
Open a method's code, and you get more elements: one typed object per instruction.

13
00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:58.601
And it's lazy.

14
00:00:58.601 --> 00:01:01.389
A part is parsed only when you open it.

15
00:01:02.467 --> 00:01:04.304
Building runs the other way.

16
00:01:04.304 --> 00:01:07.323
You pass lambdas, and each one gets a builder.

17
00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:10.491
The CodeBuilder has one method per instruction.

18
00:01:10.491 --> 00:01:14.331
Each call emits one element, and build turns them into bytes.

19
00:01:15.233 --> 00:01:21.384
To change a class, a transform hands each element of the old class to the new class's builder.

20
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.319
Pass it through with builder.with, and it comes out unchanged.

21
00:01:25.533 --> 00:01:27.439
Call nothing, and it's dropped.

22
00:01:27.667 --> 00:01:31.957
A code transform does the same with instructions.

23
00:01:31.957 --> 00:01:38.261
Call other builder methods, and you replace it: ldc 290 becomes ldc 310.

24
00:01:38.467 --> 00:01:44.606
It's a flatMap: each element in, zero, one or more out.

25
00:01:44.606 --> 00:01:50.299
FeeRuleV2's fee now returns 3,875 instead of 3,625.

26
00:01:51.467 --> 00:01:55.776
In code, it's a lambda that gets the builder and one element.

27
00:01:55.776 --> 00:01:57.965
Kotlin calls the same Java API.

28
00:01:59.133 --> 00:01:59.781
The rule.

29
00:01:59.781 --> 00:02:02.227
A class is a sequence of elements.

30
00:02:02.227 --> 00:02:08.199
Parse reads them, build writes them, and a transform maps each one to zero or more.

31
00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:09.529
Quick check.

32
00:02:09.529 --> 00:02:16.112
For ldc 290, a code transform calls with on the element, then ldc 310.

33
00:02:16.112 --> 00:02:18.745
What does the new code hold?

34
00:02:21.133 --> 00:02:21.800
Both.

35
00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:24.735
ldc 290, then ldc 310.

36
00:02:24.735 --> 00:02:27.937
One element in, two out.
